{"cases":[{"attack":true,"case":"The target's core mechanism reframes preference as a 'risk-maximizing strategy' to choose the theory with the 'greatest capacity for future falsification.' This directly contradicts the problem context which notes that scientists (and Popper) prefer better-corroborated theories when 'choosing which theory to act on.' In practical application (acting), agents seek reliability and predictive success, not vulnerability. A theory selected specifically because it is 'most informative' and thus 'most likely to fail next' is irrational for guiding action (e.g., building a bridge or administering medicine). The target fails to explain how maximizing the chance of future error constitutes a defensible basis for present action, thereby smuggling in an unstated assumption that the theory will *not* fail immediately, which re-introduces the very inductive confidence the argument claims to reject.","premise":"Acting on a theory requires an expectation of its short-term reliability, whereas the target advocates selecting theories based on their high probability of future falsification.","successor_question":"If the chosen theory is selected for its high likelihood of being falsified next, what rational basis does the agent have for relying on it for any immediate practical decision?","target":"9f667af5b4cfb931e7a50f5fcb4c95b6e57327052235ce24fe4cc76b8620c74f"}]}